  "body": "I think there is an inconsistency in the spec vs. browser implementation as far as the permissibility of cloning `Object.prototype`.\r\n\r\nPer the cloning step 23, at https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#safe-passing-of-structured-data :\r\n\r\n> Otherwise, if value is an exotic object, then throw a \"DataCloneError\" DOMException.\r\n\r\nThere are previous steps to handle, e.g., an Array exotic object, but `Object.prototype` itself, which is defined as being \"exotic\" as well\\*, does not appear to be handled by the previous steps, so it appears it should throw the cloning exception.\r\n\r\nHowever, as per https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=699831 (see also https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=698564#c1 ), Chromium has no issues with the likes of `postMessage` passing `Object.prototype` directly. Same with Firefox.\r\n\r\nIs this an issue for the browsers to fix or should the spec be changed here?\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\n\\* From 19.1.3 at https://tc39.github.io/ecma262/#sec-properties-of-the-object-prototype-object:\r\n\r\n> The Object prototype object is an immutable prototype exotic object.\r\n\r\nwhere an \"immutable prototype exotic object\" (which is only used, at least within the spec, to refer to `Object.prototype`) is defined in section 9.4.7 at https://tc39.github.io/ecma262/#sec-immutable-prototype-exotic-objects as follows:\r\n\r\n> An immutable prototype exotic object is an exotic object that has a [[Prototype]] internal slot that will not change once it is initialized.",
